    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 the suite contains a single acceptance test (`coreCountsNamesakes`), and the full `./mvnw test` run is `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation. Because the acceptance test reads the value back via `GET /api/owners/{id}` (not just the create response), the count is computed at create time and **persisted** on the owner, mirroring how the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` derived fields work.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` as a `readOnly` `int32` property (min 0) to the `Owner` response schema. This regenerates `OwnerDto` with a `namesakeCount` accessor during the build.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter. MapStruct auto-maps it into `OwnerDto` by name, so it's returned on both the create response and later GETs.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, before saving, count existing owners whose `firstName` **and** `lastName` match the new owner case-insensitively (via `findAllOwners()`, consistent with the existing duplicate/customer-code logic), and set it on the entity.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`db/hsqldb`, `db/h2`, `db/mysql`, `db/postgres`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column. Tests run on the `hsqldb` profile; the others are updated for consistency.\n\nThe test creates two owners sharing `firstName`+`lastName` (different addresses so no household 409), and the second reports `namesakeCount = 1`, which passes.",
